```

In the same target, `Parser.parse` must be callable off the main actor. If `Parser` is a stateless namespace, declare it as `nonisolated enum Parser`. `Item` must be `Sendable` because `[Item]` crosses back to the main actor; for a plain value type, `nonisolated struct Item: Sendable` is a typical fit.

In SwiftUI, call it with `.task { await model.load() }` so the view owns cancellation. The cancellation check after parsing prevents a cancelled load from publishing results; it doesn’t interrupt the synchronous parser itself.

Use `@Observable` for UI state and make `load()` async so the caller owns the task. Assuming `feedURL` is an HTTP(S) URL and `Parser.parse` is fast, keep parsing on the main actor for now: `URLSession` handles the network I/O without blocking it.

```swift
import Foundation
import Observation

@Observable
final class FeedModel {
    private(set) var items: [Item] = []
    private(set) var isLoading = false
    private(set) var loadError: (any Error)?

    private let feedURL: URL

    init(feedURL: URL) {
        self.feedURL = feedURL
    }

    func load() async {
        guard !isLoading, !Task.isCancelled else { return }

        isLoading = true
        loadError = nil
        defer { isLoading = false }

        do {
            let (data, response) = try await URLSession.shared.data(from: feedURL)
            try Task.checkCancellation()

            guard let http = response as? HTTPURLResponse else {
                throw URLError(.badServerResponse)
            }
            guard (200..<300).contains(http.statusCode) else {
                throw FeedError.httpStatus(http.statusCode)
            }

            let parsed = Parser.parse(data)
            try Task.checkCancellation()
            items = parsed
        } catch {
            // URLSession cancellation can surface as URLError.cancelled.
            if !Task.isCancelled {
                loadError = error
            }
        }
    }

    private enum FeedError: Error {
        case httpStatus(Int)
    }
}
```

In SwiftUI, attach `.task { await model.load() }` to the feed view so SwiftUI can cancel the load when the view disappears. The model inherits main-actor isolation from the target setting, so it needs neither an explicit `@MainActor` nor a dispatch back to the main queue. The `isLoading` guard ignores overlapping calls, and a failed refresh leaves the existing items intact.

If Instruments shows that parsing causes a UI hitch, move just the parsing into an `@concurrent` async helper. Make the parser callable off the main actor and use `Sendable` item values for the result that crosses back. Simply marking the parser `async` won’t move it off the main actor in this setup.

